              "blocks": {}
            },
            "heading": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<h2>What the light actually does</h2>",
                "type_preset": "h4",
                "width": "100%",
                "alignment": "left"
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "body": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>Low-level light therapy puts two wavelengths on the scalp: visible red at around 660&nbsp;nm, which acts at the surface, and near-infrared at around 850&nbsp;nm, a longer wavelength that reaches slightly deeper. Light at these wavelengths is absorbed by cells and supports normal cellular activity in the skin and follicles.</p><p>It is not a heat treatment and it is not a surgical laser. It does not hurt. Most people feel nothing beyond gentle warmth from the infrared setting.</p>",
                "type_preset": "rte",
                "width": "100%",
                "alignment": "left",
                "max_width": "narrow"
              },
              "blocks": {}
            }
          },
          "block_order": [
            "caption",
            "heading",
            "body"
          ]
        }
